<<^ code=`&#x0001;` from=`?` to=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store`>>
<<^ code=`&#x0002;`>>

```toml meta
cacheable = true
hydrate   = true
l-space   = "stable"
role      = "the sealed store's one private-byte format as law — the household promise and its limits; the key table (who holds each key, what it seals, where it rests, what unlock needs, what a carrier holds, what removal and recovery do); the sealed value's bytes, its associated data and its named refusals; the address rule; the key version, lockbox, manifest, checkpoint, hand-over, memory-import and re-seal records; the lockbox store, the chunk index and the content-key holder with its memory section and its import door; the epoch rule at removal and at admission, and the same-face reading at a recovery; the sibling-branch rule; what a blind carrier holds and learns; what the code holds and what the later steps owe"
title     = "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store"
type      = "text/memetic-wikitext+tiddlywiki"
uri-path  = "ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/sealed-store"
```

<<~ kanawai uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/ref/RFC-2119`>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/head`>>

! The sealed store ~ one format for every private byte

Cleartext lives in two places alone: the live wiki in memory, and the files the operator projects to disk. Every other private byte rests as an ''authenticated sealed value'', and the same bytes serve at rest on a reader's own disk and in the hands of a carrier that holds no key. Carriage therefore comes down to copying.

This carrier states that format: the keys, the sealed value, its address, the records that name and order sealed values, and the rules by which a bag's key changes. The format changes once, here. A vessel whose bytes another format sealed re-founds; no reader relabels a body of another format as authenticated.

> ''Register.'' The operator approved the model and ruled its scope, the history rule at admission and the mutual-removal ending (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/the-road`>>, the sealed store). The house settled the bytes, the record shapes and each reading marked below under those rulings.

''Scope'' (operator ruling). The format covers the wiki content stores, node and browser, and the content-addressed store, in carriage as well as at rest. The memory palace, the sensorium, receipts and logs stand outside it. Public and crossroads content keeps its plaintext address and stays readable to whoever holds the public keys or lookups.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-promise`>>

!! The household promise ~ in plain words, with its limits

* What a person writes in a private bag rests locked everywhere except in the live wiki and in that person's own projected files.
* A machine that carries a household's sealed values reads none of them. It cannot swap one value for another, cut one short, or re-order a sequence without the household's own devices refusing the result by name.
* A locked node rests sealed, even where it holds every capability: its keys open only after a human unlocks it.
* A person removed from a bag keeps what that person already read. Nothing written after the removal opens for them.
* A newcomer admitted from now reads nothing written before. A newcomer admitted with the past reads only the past the bag declared shareable when its writers wrote it.

''The limits, stated as plainly.''

* Whoever holds an unlocked device, or the projected files, reads everything that device reads.
* A carrier learns shape: how many sealed values a bag holds, their sizes and kinds, when they arrive, how they link, when a key rolls and when two key branches stand side by side. A re-seal record, by its kind alone, shows that the version it rides under follows a removal, and its clear part links the replaced address to the replacement's. It learns no name, no title, no text and no writer's key. The links a manifest cites can still tell one writer's rhythm from another's, and a bag carried at two places shows the same locator and the same addresses at both.
* A carrier may withhold a value or serve an earlier record. Withholding reads as absence and proves nothing; no reader takes a missing value as a deletion.
* A content key opens every value sealed under its version. A key that leaks opens that whole version, and a roll alone ends the exposure for later writing.
* ''A backup bundle carries content keys off the device.'' Whoever opens one reads every surviving value sealed under the versions it carries, values written after its cut included, until a roll ends that version's use (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/a-thief`>>).
* A capable writer may write nonsense, may sign a checkpoint that lies, and may hand any plaintext or key it holds to anyone. The signature names the writer; the format detects no lie a holder of the full history cannot recompute.
* Present values may themselves quote text written long before. A fresh base drops the history of edits, never the words a page still shows.
* A removed reader keeps every byte and every key it held. No act recalls them (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/keys`>>).
* ''A removal binds each vessel from the moment that vessel counts the raise.'' No barrier holds every vessel at once: the house keeps no global now. A vessel that has not yet read a raise may still wrap a key for the removed reader, or hand a span over to it. Once that vessel counts the raise it rolls, and nothing it seals afterwards opens for that reader.
* ''A roll leaves out the seat it can name.'' A removed device leaves by the seat its own key names. The vessel that founded a face's key group holds that group through a veil identity no device edge names, so a raise over the founding device finds no seat to leave out, and that vessel keeps receiving keys until a fresh group stands (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/keys`>>).
* ''A remaining reader counts a removal's version only where its own ledger counts the raise.'' Until its board carries the raise, the successor's lockbox opens for it and delivers nothing.
* The lockbox rests on Keyhive's membership, an alpha library the house treats as a seat for keys and never yet as a proven boundary.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/keys`>>

!! The key table

|! key |! who holds it |! what it seals or signs |! where it rests |! what unlock needs |! what a carrier holds |! what removal and recovery do |
| ''S key'' | one vessel, inside its keyslots | the custody root, the identity records and the vessel's Keyhive archive | wrapped in each keyslot | a human route: passphrase or paper; embers where bound | nothing | an unbind offers a rotation; paper or passphrase recovers it (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor`>>) |
| ''Keyhive member keys'' | each vessel seated in a bag's key group | the lockboxes that carry content keys to that group | inside the Keyhive archive, sealed under the S key on a node | the S key | nothing: a wrap's public operations name its readers and travel the member lane alone (#/the-records, the lockbox) | a counted raise revokes the seat; a lift seats afresh and returns no earlier key (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/re-admission`>>) |
| ''Content key, one per key version'' | every reader the bag's key group held when a lockbox for that version got wrapped | every sealed value of that version: changes, checkpoints, blobs and the private part of each record | wrapped in a lockbox; bare in the content-key holder's memory alone, and only while the vessel stands unlocked (#/the-store); sealed inside a backup bundle's memory section wherever a person cut one | the S key, then the Keyhive seat, then the lockbox | the lockbox as ciphertext, and the version's opaque id | a removal mints a successor version; the removed reader keeps the versions it held; a recovered device receives lockboxes as any newcomer does, and imports the versions its own face's bundle carries (#/the-store) |
| ''Bundle key, one per cut'' | the person who holds the bundle's route | one face's backup bundle: its root, its lines and its memory section | wrapped in the bundle's own slot tree, inside the bundle file | the bundle's route: passphrase or paper | nothing | a removal reaches no bundle; a lost route closes its bundle for good (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-bundle`>>) |
| ''Outbox key, one per vessel'' | one vessel's daemon island, for the life of an unlock | each parcel in the vessel's own outbox, sealed whole under randomized authenticated encryption | wrapped under the S key; the island holds it bare in memory alone while the vessel stands unlocked | the S key | nothing: no parcel leaves its vessel but by the member lane | an S-key rotation re-wraps it and re-seals no parcel (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-table`>>) |
| ''Body key, one per sealed value'' | nobody: each side derives it | the segments of one sealed value | nowhere | the content key | nothing | follows its content key |
| ''Walker's carry key, one per carried document'' | the devices that hold one walker's per-Nexus leaf | one document that walker hands a hearth to carry | the leaf seed and the document's digest derive it, and that document's receipt in the walker's own walk store keeps a copy | the leaf seed, or the receipt | the sealed value alone | a fresh leaf opens a fresh carriage; no hearth act reaches a key, and one exposed receipt opens one document |
| ''Session signing key'' | one live session, beneath its vessel's face-scoped key | manifests, checkpoints and key version records; it seals nothing | in the session's memory alone | a live session (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-key-hierarchy`>>) | nothing: every signature rides inside a sealed part | a withdrawal ends the vessel's face-scoped key; a reader then refuses its later records |
| ''No key'' | the world | public and crossroads content, in the clear under a plaintext address | the public shelf | nothing | the bytes | nothing to roll |

''One content key serves one bag at one key version.'' A vessel mints it from 32 fresh random bytes and never installs one key under two bags. No content key derives from content, from a Nexus secret or from another bag's key.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-sealed-value`>>

!! The sealed value ~ its bytes, its associated data, its refusals

''Private bytes take randomized authenticated encryption.'' Sealing the same body twice yields two unlike values under two unlike addresses. Convergent sealing, where content derives its own key, leaves the private tier: it lets any insider confirm a guessed body against a held address, and it bought little deduplication.

!!! The associated data

Every sealed value binds four facts, and a reader supplies all four before any byte opens:

* ''the bag'': the bag's own id, as its readers know it;
* ''the key version'': the 32-byte id of the content key (#/the-records);
* ''the role'': one of `change`, `checkpoint`, `blob` or `record`;
* ''the logical reference'': what the value stands for inside the bag. A change carries its change id, a checkpoint its checkpoint id, a blob the plaintext digest its pointer names, and a record its record id.

The four ride one canonical image: the format's own name first, then each field as a four-byte big-endian length followed by its bytes. No two field tuples share an image. A bag id, like every id a record names in text, reads as well-formed text: text that would lose a character in its own encoding names nothing. The SHA-256 digest of that image stands as the ''context digest''.

!!! The bytes

| offset | width | field |
| 0 | 4 | the format mark: the first four bytes of SHA-256 over the format's name. A changed format takes a new name and so a new mark; the mark holds no counter, and the registry of names refuses a name whose mark equals a standing one |
| 4 | 16 | the version hint: the first sixteen bytes of the key version id |
| 20 | 32 | the salt, drawn at random for this value |
| 52 | 32 | the commitment |
| 84 | … | the segments, in order |

* ''The body key'' derives by HKDF-SHA-256 from the content key, with the salt as salt and the context digest inside the `info`, under a name of its own.
* ''The commitment'' derives the same way under a second name. It binds the content key, the salt and all four facts. A reader recomputes it before any segment opens, so no sealed value opens under two keys or under two contexts.
* ''A segment'' holds up to 65,536 plaintext bytes under ChaCha20-Poly1305 with the body key. Its nonce carries an eleven-byte big-endian counter and one last byte that reads 1 on the final segment and 0 elsewhere; the context digest rides as its associated data. Every segment but the last holds exactly 65,536 bytes. The last may hold fewer, and holds none only where the whole body holds none.
* ''A reader cuts segments from the front'': 65,552 sealed bytes each, and whatever remains forms the last segment. A segment shorter than its 16-byte tag refuses. A sealed value holds fewer than 2^88 segments, and a body that would need more refuses at the seal.
* The salt makes each body key single-use, so a counter nonce never repeats under one key and no writer shares a counter with another.

!!! The refusals

An opener answers with the plaintext or with exactly one named refusal, never a boolean and never partial bytes:

|! refusal |! what the reader learned |
| `address-unauthorized` | no named source vouched for the address |
| `envelope-malformed` | too short to hold a header and one segment |
| `address-mismatch` | the bytes do not hash to the address an authorized source named: altered or swapped |
| `format-unknown` | sealed bytes of a format this reader does not hold; never read as cleartext |
| `key-mismatch` | the key in hand does not belong to the key version the reader named |
| `version-mismatch` | the value's version hint names another key version |
| `context-mismatch` | the commitment fails: sealed for another bag, role or reference, or by a hand that never held this key |
| `sequence-broken` | a segment fails its tag: altered, out of order, repeated, cut inside, or trailing the final one |
| `truncated` | the bytes end on a whole segment that names itself as not the last |

The checks run in that order, and the commitment check precedes every decryption.

An opener takes an ''authorized address'' and never a bare one, and the caller names the source that vouched for it. Three sources stand: a verified manifest entry (#/the-records); a receipt the reader wrote for a value it sealed with its own hands; and a record that carries its own sealed part, where the context binds that part to the record's id and the signature inside answers for the rest.

''A blob's reader checks one thing more.'' A blob's logical reference names the SHA-256 digest of its plaintext, the same digest its pointer carries. The reader hashes what opened and refuses bytes that name another digest (`plaintext-mismatch`).

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-address`>>

!! The address rule

* ''A sealed value's address hashes its ciphertext'': BLAKE3 over every byte of the sealed value, the header included, spelled `blake3:` and the hex digest.
* ''Clear public content keeps its plaintext address'': SHA-256 over the raw bytes, spelled as today.
* A holder of no key recomputes a sealed address from the bytes alone. That check proves the bytes match the address, and proves nothing about whether the address belongs in a bag: the authorized address and the commitment carry that.
* A carrier stores, serves and verifies by address. It never needs, and never receives, the four facts.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-records`>>

!! The records ~ key version, lockbox, manifest, checkpoint

''Each signed record holds a clear part and a sealed part.'' The clear part carries what a carrier needs to index and nothing that names a person. The sealed part carries the record's private fields, its writer's session certificate and the signature, as a sealed value of role `record` under the record's own key version. A record's id hashes its clear part under the record kind's own name.

''A capable writer signs.'' A session signs each record with its session key under the `act` purpose, beneath the vessel's face-scoped key (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof`>>). A reader trusts a record only after six checks pass, in order: the clear part reads whole in its kind's own shape; it names the reader's bag locator, and the key in hand stands as its key version; the sealed part opens under that key version and the record's id; the private fields and the session certificate read whole; the signature covers the record's kind, its id and its private fields; and the certificate's face holds write standing in the bag by the reader's own reading. The lineage then judges a key version's transition. A reader keeps each record it counted, and a record that goes missing later changes no reading it already took.

!!! The bag locator

A carrier names a bag by an opaque ''locator'': a keyed digest of the bag's id under the bag's founding content key. Records carry the locator and never the bag's id. A reader admitted later never holds the founding key, so every lockbox carries the locator beside the key, and a reader refuses a record whose locator differs from the one its lockbox named.

!!! The key version record

* ''The key version id'' derives from the content key and the bag's id by HKDF-SHA-256. Whoever holds a key recomputes the id; nobody finds a second key for one id. The id commits a key drawn at random and hides nothing about a key someone could guess. A lockbox therefore needs no signature of its own.
* ''Clear'': the locator, the key version id, the ids of its parent versions, and the id of the span it belongs to (#/epochs).
* ''Sealed'': the cause (`founding`, `removal`, `admission` or `join`, with the id of the causal act where one applies), the span's history declaration, the join proofs, the readers its mint could not serve, the certificate and the signature.
* Two unlike records under one key version id refuse both, and every version that descends from that id leaves the lineage's readings with them, whatever order the records arrived in. The conflict shows at every door, and the bag continues only from a version that descends from neither.
* ''A reader compares the records it verified under one id before it judges any transition.'' A record that fails that reader's own evidence still conflicts with its unlike twin: neither counts, so no reader's ledger and no arrival order picks one of the two. Records that state one fact under one id conflict with nothing.
* ''A reader counts a successor only where its transition verifies.'' Every parent whose key the reader holds already stands counted in the reader's lineage. The span rule of #/epochs holds. A `removal` cites one or more raises, and the reader's own ledger counts every one; an `admission` cites an admission act the reader verifies. A `join` cites two or more versions, and its sealed part carries one proof per parent: a keyed digest of the new version's id under that parent's key, which a reader checks for every parent whose key it holds.
* ''A reader that holds no key for a parent counts the version as a base of its own reading'' (house reading). A newcomer admitted from now, a reader handed one span, and a reader a sibling's mint never served each hold a version whose parents they cannot open. Such a reader counts the version where its own record verifies under the key its lockbox delivered and every rule its counted versions let it check holds: the cause, the proofs for the parents it does hold, and the span's declaration wherever it already counted a version of that span. It reads nothing sealed under a parent it holds no key for.
* ''A base needs a license the reader itself verifies.'' A removal's raise counts in the reader's own ledger. An admission names an act the reader verifies. A join proves one or more parents the reader holds. A version that offers none of these counts nowhere: no record another writer signed stands in for a reader's own evidence, a hand-over record included.
* ''A base lasts only while the reader holds no key for that parent.'' Once a lockbox delivers the parent's key, the reader counts its lineage afresh, and the version stands only where that parent then counts. A parent whose key the reader holds must stand counted first, as before.
* ''A base carries no authority over a span's past.'' A reader hands a span over only where it counted that span's opener, since the opener alone states the declaration its span opened with.
* ''A key enters use only under a key version record its reader counted.'' A lockbox that opens to a key no counted record names delivers nothing.

!!! The lockbox

* A lockbox carries one content key to the readers of one bag at one key version. Its payload holds the bag's id, the bag's locator, the key version id and the key. Its clear part names the locator and the key version id.
* ''The lockbox wraps to Keyhive members through Keyhive's own encryption'' (house reading). Keyhive keeps a group's membership private and converges a removal across replicas; the house key adds what Keyhive's own chunk keys lack: associated data, a commitment, a format that outlives a Keyhive version move, and an epoch a writer can hand over whole. A sealed value never rides Keyhive's chunk encryption.
* ''Each lockbox wraps into a Keyhive document of its own'' (house reading). The wrapping vessel mints a fresh document, seats in it exactly the readers that lockbox serves, and encrypts the payload once. That document takes no later member and seals nothing else. Three measured facts force the shape (`packages/lararium-keyhive/tests/seat-roll-substrate.probe.test.ts`, `lockbox-wrap.test.ts`). A revocation on a sentinel document rolls no key of a bag delegated to it, so a wrap through the bag's own document still opens for a removed reader. One Keyhive chunk key opens the keys before it. A member seated in a document opens nothing that document sealed before the seat. A fresh document holds a leaf for each seated reader and for nobody else, so no removal has to reach it and no later seat finds an earlier key in it.
* ''A lockbox names no predecessor.'' Each lockbox stands alone, under a content reference of its own that cites none.
* ''Whom a wrap serves'' (house reading). The wrapping vessel reads the bag's key group as its own replica holds it at the moment of the wrap. It leaves out the seat a roll names and every party its own edge reading sets aside, whether or not the key group still seats them.
* ''A reader the mint could not serve stays owed, by name, inside the record.'' A wrap seats a reader by a contact card the vessel holds. A reader whose card it lacks goes unserved, and the mint stands for the rest. The key version record names each unserved reader in its sealed part, so every reader of that version reads the debt and no carrier does. A holder that later holds the card wraps that same key for that reader alone, after a fresh reading: the reader still holds its seat and its standing.
* ''The debt ends where a roll names that reader's seat.'' A vessel that takes the roll rests a note for the bag and that reader before it plans a successor, whether or not it holds a version of the bag. The note holds a digest of each raise the roll answered and no name. From then on the vessel owes that reader only a version that follows a successor for every one of those raises: a version minted earlier carries no debt, and neither does one that reaches the vessel later. A seat that returns under the same identifier stands fresh and receives no earlier key (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/re-admission`>>). A note that could not rest leaves the roll owed.
* ''A wrap answers only once it can open again.'' The wrapping identity opens its own wrap from an archive taken after the wrap and from nothing earlier, so the wrap's public operations and that identity's archive both reach rest before the lockbox does. A write that fails there fails the wrap.
* ''The box'' holds the wrap document's id and the Keyhive ciphertext, and nothing else. Both read opaque, so a lockbox rests beside the sealed values and travels to a carrier as they do.
* ''The wrap's public Keyhive operations name its readers' Keyhive identifiers.'' They never ride to a carrier. They travel the key group's own member lane inside a ''parcel'': one lockbox, its key version record and those operations, for one reader.
* ''Keyhive binds no associated data, so the payload's own image binds the lockbox.'' A reader that opens a lockbox recomputes the key version id from the key and the bag's id. It refuses a payload whose locator or key version differs from the lockbox's clear part, and a payload that names another locator than the one the reader already holds for that founding. A box moved under another lockbox's clear part therefore refuses by name.
* ''A lockbox proves no standing.'' An earlier lockbox still opens for the reader it served, and that reader keeps the version it held; nothing sealed under a later version opens with it.

!!! The manifest

* A manifest lists sealed values a writer added to one bag under one key version. ''Clear'': the locator, the key version id, the ids of the manifests its writer had read, and its entries. ''Sealed'': the certificate and the signature.
* ''An entry'' carries the role, the address, an entry locator and its parent addresses. A change names the addresses of the values that hold its causal parents; a checkpoint names the addresses it covers; a blob names none.
* ''The entry locator'' derives from the content key and the entry's context digest. A reader that wants a logical reference computes its locator and finds the address; a carrier reads an opaque value. The manifest serves as the bag's map from logical name to address, so a pointer in the wiki names the plaintext digest and never a ciphertext address.
* ''A verified entry authorizes its address'' for exactly the bag, key version, role and reference whose locator matched.
* Manifests accrete. A writer cites the manifests it read, no counter orders two writers, and no manifest stands as the latest.

!!! The checkpoint

* A checkpoint record names one sealed value of role `checkpoint`. ''Clear'': the locator, the key version id, the span id, the base kind, the address, and the addresses it covers. ''Sealed'': the causal frontier in the document engine's own ids, the certificate and the signature.
* ''What a checkpoint asserts.'' Its signer asserts that the value at the address opens to a whole document state; that the state's causal frontier equals the sealed frontier; and that the state includes every change the covered values carry. A `fresh` base asserts one thing more: the state holds present values alone and no operation from before it.
* ''A fresh base founds a new document from present values.'' Its writer never saves the earlier document under a new name. A reader refuses a fresh base whose opened document holds more than its own founding change.
* ''The admitting vessel answers for a fresh base'': it cuts the base itself, or recomputes one another writer cut, before it wraps a lockbox for the newcomer.
* A reader that holds the covered history recomputes the state and compares; a state that differs refuses the checkpoint and shows the conflict at every door. A reader that lacks the history relies on the signer's standing and on the vessel that admitted it, and the record names that signer.
* The frontier stays sealed: a change id hashes plaintext, and a carrier that read one could confirm a guess.

!!! The hand-over record

* A hand-over record states that one holder handed one span's keys to one reader (#/epochs). ''Clear'': the locator, the key version id of the head it rides under, the span id, and the ids of the versions handed. ''Sealed'': the reader's Keyhive identifier, the certificate and the signature.
* Every reader of that head verifies it as any record, and so learns who received the span's past and from whom. The record audits the act. It moves no reader's count of a version.

!!! The memory-import record

* A memory-import record states that one seat came to hold one key version by recovery: its vessel imported that version's key from its own face's backup bundle (#/the-store). ''Clear'': the locator and the key version id. ''Sealed'': the seat's identifier on the key shore, the certificate and the signature. It rides under the version it names, so every reader of that version verifies it as any record.
* ''The record grants no standing and no version by itself.'' It tells readers of that version which seat holds it by recovery. A reader's count of the version moves by the lineage's own rules alone.
* ''A hand-over record would misstate the act.'' A hand-over names a holder that handed a key on. A recovery names no such hand, so the import signs a kind of its own.

!!! The re-seal record

* A re-seal record states that one replacement moved one value to the version this vessel writes under, from a version the roll it answers rolled past (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/rulings`>>, the re-seal proof). ''The roll it answers'' stands as the nearest removal within the writing version's span, that version itself first, so a join or a later write after the removal still owes the replacement; a span with no removal owes none. ''The span fence'' (#/epochs): the replaced version stands in the same span, so a replacement never carries a body past a from-now admission. Where the body rests under several versions the roll rolled past, the newest hands it on: one no other of them descends from, the lowest id among equals. ''Clear'': the locator, the writing version's key version id (the version it rides under), the replaced version's id, the replaced value's address, the replacement's address, and the entry locator the replaced version's manifest names the value by. ''Sealed'': the removal acts that authorized the roll, the replaced entry's role and logical reference, the certificate and the signature. The face session that writes the replacement signs one record per replacement, so a reader offline through the roll learns which value replaced which and whose hand re-sealed it.
* ''A reader with partial history verifies what it holds.'' The record reads as any record. Its bindings then read against the reader's own counted lineage, the keys of counted versions and the manifests it verified, and a version it verified and never counted binds nothing: the nearest removal within the successor's span names every act the record cites; the replaced version stands in that span and upstream of that removal; the record's role and reference recompute its entry under the replaced key, and a manifest under the replaced version names the old address under that entry; the same role and reference recompute the replacement's entry under the successor's key, and a manifest the re-seal's own signer wrote names the new address under it. A binding the reader holds the history for and that breaks refuses `reseal-unbound`; one whose history or key it lacks reads unverified, never complete.
* ''A replacement leaves the replaced value at rest.'' A removed reader keeps what it already read; the replacement opens for the successor's readers alone. ''The record rests before the manifest'' that names the replacement, so a stop between leaves a replacement every reader reads unverified, and the next try finishes that one; a body the roll's side already names refuses `value-current`, so no retry duplicates a replacement.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-store`>>

!! The lockbox store, the chunk index and the content-key holder

!!! What rests, and where

* ''The store keeps lockboxes, signed records and the chunk index's entries, and none of them holds a secret, a name or a key in the clear.'' A lockbox holds Keyhive ciphertext and opaque ids. A record holds a clear part of opaque ids and a sealed part. An index entry holds opaque ids and addresses. They rest as plain bytes beside the sealed values, each under a name its own bytes derive, and a carrier may hold every one of them.
* ''The outbox alone stays on its vessel, sealed.'' A parcel a vessel wrapped rests in its own outbox until the member lane takes it. A parcel holds no key; it holds Keyhive operations that name readers, so no carrier takes it and no verb answers one. Each parcel seals whole under the outbox key, a key the vessel key wraps, and rests under a random name with its reader and its routing inside the sealed body, so a locked disk names no reader; its crash law reads at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-table`>> (house ruling under the operator's delegation).
* ''No reader empties the outbox until step four.'' The member lane that takes a parcel to its reader arrives with the carriage protocol (#/owed). Until it stands, a parcel rests in the outbox and travels nowhere, and no path and no verb reads one out.
* ''The chunk index'' keeps one index entry for each entry of each manifest the vessel stored, under a name that leads with the bag's locator, the key version and the entry locator. A holder of no key answers what a vessel holds for a locator at a version from those names alone. A reader that holds the bag's key derives the entry locator for a logical reference and finds its index entry with nothing opened.
* ''An index entry authorizes nothing.'' An address opens only through the verified manifest the index entry names, so an index entry altered at rest finds no entry and opens no byte.
* ''A name never changes its bytes.'' The store writes each value whole and once, every byte of it however many calls the file system takes, so a restart finds what a vessel held and holds no second copy of it.
* ''The provider's own Keyhive chunks'' rest the same way: ciphertext under an opaque name, written through before the call that sealed it returns. A creator and a reader that stand again each find what they held.

!!! The content-key holder

* ''One holder keeps bare content keys, in memory alone, and only while the vessel stands unlocked.'' It lives where the vessel's Keyhive seat lives. No bare key crosses a worker port or reaches disk, a log, a receipt or an answer: a caller hands the holder a body and takes back a sealed value, or hands it a reference and takes back the body. A cut takes back a memory section sealed under its bundle's key, and nothing else leaves (below).
* ''At unlock the holder opens every lockbox its Keyhive seat opens.'' It reads each payload, and counts each key version record under the key that payload delivered. Nothing private rests for it: it rebuilds every bag id, locator and lineage from its lockboxes. ''At lock it overwrites every key and forgets every reading'', and every ask then refuses by name.
* ''A key reaches rest before any value seals under it.'' A mint wraps and stores the lockbox, signs the key version record and counts the version, then stores the record. A stop before the record rests leaves a lockbox no record names: it delivers nothing, and the next mint draws a fresh key. No stop leaves a sealed value whose key no lockbox holds, and none leaves one key version stored twice.
* ''The first key'' (house reading). A bag's first private value mints its founding version. The session that seals signs the founding record, and the caller declares the span's history at that moment. A founding with no declaration stands closed: no later act can add a declaration its writers never made.
* ''A vessel under its own shadow performs no key act.'' It founds nothing, mints nothing, joins nothing, hands nothing over, hands no parcel on and serves no reader, so no wrap of its own ever seats it and no key leaves by its hand.
* ''Which acts the shadow stops'' (house reading). A key act wraps a key or hands one on (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/keys`>>). Four acts sit beside that line, and each takes its own answer.
** ''The parcels for a reader refuse by name'' (`own-edge-shadowed`). A parcel carries a lockbox to a reader, so a vessel that hands one on hands a key on.
** ''A cut of the memory section and an import each refuse by name'' (`own-edge-shadowed`). The cut hands keys beyond the holder, and the import wraps a key for the vessel's own seat.
** ''A seal under a head the vessel already holds runs.'' It draws no key and wraps for nobody, and the readers it reaches stay the readers that head already served. Each reader's own standing check judges the manifest the seal signs. A seal that would found a bag or join heads mints, and so refuses.
** ''A receipt runs.'' The vessel rests what another hand wrapped for it and counts again. The hand that wrapped answered for whom it served, and a refusal here would withhold nothing the vessel does not already hold.
** ''The choice of a head runs.'' It moves the vessel's own notes and nothing else.
* ''An act reads the vessel's shadow once, at its entry.'' The holder runs one act at a time. A raise the vessel counts while an act runs binds its next act: the house keeps no barrier across a fold and an act in flight (#/the-promise).
* ''A lock ends an act in flight.'' The holder checks its lock before each write to rest and before each answer, so a lock that lands between two writes of one act stops every later write. What the earlier writes left reads as any stop does: a lockbox no record names delivers nothing, and a value no manifest names opens for nobody.
* ''Rival foundings'' (house reading). Two vessels may each found one bag before either reads the other. Each founding names a locator of its own, and the two lineages stay apart under those locators. A vessel that opens a lockbox of a founding it never counted keeps that lineage beside its own, reads what stands sealed under it, shows the rival founding at every door, and keeps writing under the founding it wrote under. Where it wrote under none, a person chooses. No clock, no arrival order and no id comparison picks one.
* ''A removal rolls every founding a vessel holds a head in.'' The roll keys its work on the bag and the founding's locator. A founding that reaches a vessel after a raise settled still owes that raise its roll: the vessel hands its kept rolls to the content-key roll again whenever a lineage or a head arrives, and a roll already answered there settles as not owed.
* ''Every sealed value enters a manifest.'' The session that seals signs a manifest for what it added, and the index takes that manifest's entries. A reader opens a value by its logical reference: it derives the entry locator, finds the index entry, verifies the manifest the entry names, and opens the address that manifest authorizes.
* ''One manifest for each write batch'' (house reading). A seal of a blob, or of a batch of the document store's values, signs one manifest, and its parents name the heads of what this vessel verified under the same key version: the verified manifests no other verified manifest cites. Each head names its own parents, so the heads carry every manifest the writer read, and a seal's work holds flat however long the bag's ledger grows; the counted heads stay true because a manifest reaches the rest by two doors alone: the vessel's own seal moves them, and `receive()` takes the reading afresh so they count again from rest (house reading, lane `b5b`, 2026-11-06: a seal that cited every manifest read the whole ledger at each write, and a long session's reads crawled to the verb's wait). Each checkpoint in a batch also signs a `continuation` checkpoint record naming the addresses it covers.
* ''The document store's values seal as `change` and `checkpoint` values'' (house reading). An incremental save seals as a change and a snapshot as a checkpoint, each under the logical reference its storage key names. A change names as its parents the addresses of the values its store already held for that document; a checkpoint names them as its cover. The engine's own rest keeps a pointer that names the sealed address and nothing of the body, and a sealed bag rests no sync state, since a sync state carries peers' heads in the clear.
* ''Hydration answers to the session's face'' (house reading; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/session-contract#/the-document-view`>>). A key alone grants no read. Before any byte or name moves, the session must wear the face the bag belongs to (`session-face-foreign`, or `session-absent` where none stands), and a value whose authorizing manifest another face's session signed refuses (`writer-face-foreign`). A value that does not open reads as its named refusal, never as an absent value.
* ''A session signs, or nothing mints.'' A founding, a roll, a join, a manifest, a hand-over and an import each take the signer of the session that acts. Where no session signer stands, the holder refuses by name: no vessel key and no daemon key signs in a session's place.

!!! The memory section and the import door

A face's backup bundle carries the content keys its vessel held, so a face that returns on a fresh device reads its own past (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-bundle`>>). Two acts of the holder serve it.

* ''A cut seals the memory section inside the holder.'' The holder gathers each key it COUNTED AND HELD among the bags its face's composition names. It writes one entry per bag and key version: the bag's id, the bag's locator, the key version id and the key. It seals the section under the bundle key: to the public half of a key pair that key alone derives, which the cut names. It answers that ciphertext beside a list that names bags and versions and no key. No secret crosses the island's port at a cut.
* ''A seat that opens no lockbox cuts an empty section, chain or none.'' Such a seat holds nothing under any chain, so a vessel that dials no hearth, and so composes no chain, answers an empty memory section and caches no reading. A seat that opens a lockbox still reads under the vessel's chain alone, and a chainless reading there refuses by name (`chain-absent`).
* ''The cut refuses under the vessel's own shadow'' (`own-edge-shadowed`), and reads that shadow once, at its entry. A raise the vessel counts while a cut runs binds its next act.
* ''The island answers its own vessel alone.'' A cut and an import each carry the vessel key's signature over their own request, as the seat's duties do, so no other island, no session and no peer draws a section or lands one. ''One signed ask runs once'': the island keeps each request it answered for its own life and refuses the same one again (`memory-ask-spent`). A replay after a restart gains nothing: a cut seals to the key its own ask named, and an import meets no door.
* ''One door imports.'' The taking vessel hands its island the section and the pair's secret half, sealed to a public key the island named for that one import, so the secret opens inside the island alone. One door serves one import: the island drops it as the import begins. ''The door judges the act whole before any entry lands.'' A session signer stands (`signer-unavailable`). This vessel's own seat stands in the face's key group (`seat-not-standing`). The vessel reads no shadow over its own edge (`own-edge-shadowed`). The section opens to whole entries (`memory-malformed`).
* ''The door then judges each entry apart'', in this order, and answers one outcome for each:
** `key-forged`: the key does not recompute to the version id its entry names, under the bag it names. A forged or swapped key stops here.
** `held`: this vessel already counts and holds that version. Nothing changes.
** `record-unverified`: no key version record under that locator and id verifies under the key.
** `version-conflicted`: two unlike verified records stand under that id, or the lineage holds it conflicted.
** `transition-uncounted`: the record verifies, and the lineage does not count the version on this vessel's own evidence. The outcome names the lineage's own cause.
** `imported`: the entry passed, and landed.
* ''An entry that passes lands in three writes.'' The holder signs a `memory-import` record under the version and rests it (#/the-records). It wraps one lockbox for this vessel's own seat alone and rests it. It then reads its lockboxes afresh and counts again. A stop after the record and before the lockbox leaves a record of an import that never completed, and the same import runs again.
* ''The import adds and never replaces.'' It writes no lockbox over another, removes no record and moves no head. A bundle older than the holder's own reading adds only what the holder lacks.
* ''The import counts by the lineage's own rules'' (house reading). A removal's version counts where this vessel's own ledger counts its raise, and a join where a held parent proves it. A carried key whose transition this vessel cannot count stays out, by name. The bundle keeps that key, and the same import runs again once the vessel's own evidence stands.
* ''The bundle's own seal never replaces these checks.'' A thief who knows a bundle's route seals a body of their own making, so every entry answers to the records and the lineage, whatever the bundle's tag says.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/epochs`>>

!! Epochs ~ one bag name, key versions that mark its eras

A bag keeps ''one logical name'' for its whole life. Its content key rolls at causal acts and never by a clock, and each key version marks an epoch.

''A span'' names a run of epochs that share one causal base: one document history a single key holder could replay from its start. A span takes the id of the key version that opens it. ''A span declares at its opening whether its history stands shareable'', and every key version inside it carries that same declaration. No later act changes it: a writer knows, at the moment of writing, who may one day read that edit.

|! act |! what happens to the key |! what happens to the span |
| ''founding'' | the first version mints | the first span opens, with its declaration |
| ''removal'', a counted raise | a successor version mints in each span the vessel holds a head in; the removed reader receives no lockbox for it | each span continues; the document history runs on |
| ''admission from now'' | a successor version mints | a new span opens on a `fresh` checkpoint; the newcomer receives that version alone |
| ''admission with its past'' | no version mints | allowed only inside a span declared shareable; the newcomer receives a lockbox for each version of that span |
| ''join'' of sibling versions | one version mints, citing every sibling | siblings of one span continue it; siblings of unlike spans open a new span on a `fresh` checkpoint |

* ''An inviter never widens the audience for another writer's past'' (operator ruling). A span declared closed admits newcomers from now alone. An inviter chooses between history the span already declared shareable and a present-state admission.
* ''One face on a new device reads as the same audience'' (operator ruling). A replacement vessel of the SAME face, proven by a current re-admission, imports the versions that face already held, closed spans included (#/the-store). The ruling above forbids widening the AUDIENCE for another writer's past; a face that returns on a fresh device widens nothing. The import therefore asks for no history declaration, and no other reader gains a version by it.
* ''A from-now admission mints only once its fresh base stands.'' The admitting vessel draws the new version's key, cuts the fresh base under it, and only then wraps a lockbox. Where no hand cuts that base, the admission refuses by name and mints nothing.
* ''The base seals before any change'' (house reading). The document store cuts the base from its live document: a new document of present values whose opened state holds its own founding change and no other. Once the version's record rests, the base seals under that version as a `fresh` checkpoint, under its own manifest. No change seals under a span an admission opened until a `fresh` checkpoint of that span's opener verifies and its value rests (`fresh-base-unavailable`), so a stop between the record and the base leaves a span that takes no change.
* ''The hand-over stands as an explicit act'' (house reading). A holder names one reader and one head it holds. It wraps one lockbox for each version of that head's span to that reader alone, signs a hand-over record under the head (#/the-records), and hands the reader one parcel for each version. The act refuses by name: a span declared closed; a span whose opener the holder never counted; a head the holder cannot open; a reader that holds no seat in the bag's key group, or that the holder's own edge reading sets aside; and a vessel under its own shadow. The act reaches no version of an earlier span, and a holder that lacks a version's key hands over the versions it holds and names the rest.
* ''The reader counts a handed span as it counts any version.'' It opens the parcels and counts each record under the key its parcel delivered, each cause against its own ledger and its own reading of the admission acts. The hand-over record states who handed the span and to whom, for every reader of that head; it licenses nothing.
* ''A removal rolls forward only.'' The seat roll a vessel hands its subscribers stands as the one trigger (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/keys`>>), and the content key rolls on every answer the key group gave: the seat revoked, the seat gone, or a seat no hand here can revoke. Raises answered together share one successor version. Every counted raise owes one roll, whatever acts follow it, and a lift restores standing and never an earlier key. A roll a vessel could not finish stays owed, and the same seat roll comes again: a mint that failed, a bag whose head key has not reached the vessel yet, a bag it could not read. A vessel under its own shadow mints nothing and performs no key act in that scope.
* ''Present state without past needs a fresh causal base.'' The document engine keeps every operation inside one document, deleted text included, through save, sync and compaction alike. A `fresh` checkpoint therefore founds a new document from present values, and the span that opens on it shares no history with the span before.
* ''Within a span'', changes sealed under neighbouring key versions merge as one document. A reader needs each version's key for the values it reads.
* ''Across a span boundary'' no engine merge exists. A change written against the earlier span after the fresh base got cut stands as a ''late change''. Any holder of the manifests detects one: a change in the earlier span that no checkpoint's cover reaches. A writer that holds both spans' keys re-authors it onto the new base; until then it surfaces as a conflict and never drops in silence.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/siblings`>>

!! Sibling branches ~ no coordinator, no silent winner

* Two vessels that count the same raise, or two unlike raises, may each mint a successor from the same parent. Both versions stand as ''siblings''. Nothing ranks them: no clock, no arrival order and no id comparison.
* ''The heads of a bag's key lineage'' name the versions no other version cites. A lineage with more than one head shows a fork at every door.
* A writer seals new values under a head it holds. A writer that holds every head, and stands under no shadow of its own in that bag, may ''join'' them by minting one version that cites all of them. A writer that holds one head of several keeps writing under that head. A writer that holds some of several keeps the head it last wrote under; where it wrote under none, a person chooses.
* A roll cites the heads its vessel holds and no head it cannot open. Heads of unlike spans each take their own successor, so a roll never waits on a join, and both branches keep showing as a fork.
* A vessel that already holds a successor for a raise mints no second one for it.
* ''Mutual removal ends in an explicit fork'' (operator ruling). Each party's roll shuts the other out, both key branches stay, and a vessel that reads a shadow over its own edge mints no further version in that bag. Both parties leave the shared key group (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/keys`>>): a third member that counts both raises and holds both heads joins them into a version neither party receives. Where the two stand alone no join exists, and their relation continues only in a fresh realm, by an explicit fork, unless the realm's charter named a recovery council.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-lit-set`>>

!! The lit set ~ every open face seals under its own key

One passphrase may open every face (`lares vault unlock --all --face N`, which wears face N, and refuses by name, wearing no other face, where N stays shut under it); separate passphrases open one face at a time; every verb treats an open face alike, and a shut face waits by name and never blocks the rest. Three sets carry the faces of a vessel. The ''lit set'' names the faces open on it: lit at the pūlama, or open in the vessel where no pūlama stands. The ''lent set'' names the faces a pūlama lends a vessel standing by it, the faces whose records that vessel may read and sign for (the lend; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/the-worn-house#/the-pulama`>>). The ''worn face'' rides per session: a session wears one face inside the lent set, and the vessel wears none. The composition still mounts one face in its daemon island, the ''daemon mount'', until the ONE FACE band retires it; with no pūlama, the face the unlock's `--face N` names takes the mount and the AI seat acts as it (`wornOrdinal`). A command names an open face (`--face N`; a shut one refuses `face-not-open`) or, with one face open, acts as that one; with several open and none named it refuses and asks (`face-selector.ts`). The container shim remembers the last worn face (`config/last-worn-face`) and, after a stand or a restart with no pūlama, opens every face the credential opens and wears that one again, else face 0; under a pūlama the restart draws its lend instead, with no route. Each face's private documents (its daemon wiki's device slots, its daemon canon, its own catalog half) seal and save under ''that face's own key'', through a session that wears that face, and never under another member's.

* ''The face gate'' (`SealedDocumentStore`, port `openFaces`): a bag of any face in the set opens under that face; a bag of a face outside it refuses `session-face-foreign`. Under a pūlama the gate reads the lent set.
* ''The watch'' (`composeSealedRest.watch(repo, standing(documentId))`): a save books only under the session whose subject wears its bag's face; a session of another face never books it, so the save waits `save-unbooked` in memory, never sealed under another key and never written clear.
* ''The device table'' (`deviceDaemonDocs`, `LitDeviceFaces`): a device document files under its owner within the set, else held. A browser vessel reads the same table as a set of one.
* ''A lock of one face'' (`vault lock --face N`) drops N from the set: its saves wait in memory, never on disk, and every other open face keeps sealing.
* ''Unrelated keys under one passphrase.'' No face key derives from a passphrase: each face's custody key, address secret, atom seed and per-Nexus proof key mint at random; a passphrase only wraps a face's custody key inside that face's own slot tree, under a fresh scrypt salt and a random slot id per pin (`keyslot.bindSlot`). Two faces under one passphrase hold unrelated keys.

''The threat model.'' The lit set answers two threats: ''bytes at rest'' (a disk read with the vessel down reads only sealed values, each under its face's key) and ''cross-face logical leaks'' (no code path lands, books or seals one face's memory under another face's key or in another face's layer). It does not answer a ''live process compromise'': a process that holds several faces open holds several faces' keys at once, and an attacker inside that process reads them all. ''The crypto boundary stands at the daemon island'': the vessel's main thread holds no content key (the document store's door carries bodies and sealed values, never a key), and the island keeps one content-key holder per face (its Keyhive provider per atom key, `lightJoinedFace`), each in its own entry with that face's own chain and fold (`face-seat-table`). A later per-face process split moves each face's holder into an island of its own behind the same door, with no change to the sealed rest.

''Each lit face stands whole.'' A sibling face lit beside the worn one stands its own place session in the vessel's session book (its atom signs at this vessel's place), so a save of a document it owns books that session and seals under its own key; its hive registers the bags it owns while lit (`litFaceOwnBags`: the daemon wiki's device slots, its daemon canon by its relation, its catalog half, its own selves record), never a user wiki; its daemon canon stands by its relation, read off its own proved log; it keeps its own label and bases sidecar. A face-named INGEST resolves that face's own document as its target, the owner read off the control document (`lit-face-target`), and never the worn face's: a sibling's daemon canon by its relation, and a sibling's daemon wiki slots where that face's own wear named them in the control document, never minted ahead of it. ''EACH HAT ITS OWN CHAIN'': a lit sibling's session books its own face key, so the vessel's book answers that face's own chain and fold and the island seals through that face's own entry (its seat, its chain, its counted raises); a face the island holds shut refuses `face-not-open` on its own while every other face keeps sealing, and closing a face drops its entry whole. ''EACH HAT REACHED BY NAME'': the store door's `--face N` rides a session of face N; a lit sibling's session carries its own lane's meme writes as itself (`FACE_LANE_CHORES`), and the island runs `meme-put`, `meme-get`, `meme-list` and `meme-delete` from it on that face's own lane alone, its daemon canon or a daemon slot its own wear named, resolved through `lit-face-target` (`face-lane`), every answer naming the `face` it acted as; any other container refuses `face-lane-holds-its-own`, a shut face `face-not-open`, and with no `--face` the worn face stays the default. The tiddler door's verbs (`tiddler-get`, `tiddler-put`, `tiddler-delete`, `wiki-filter`, `wiki-render`, `wiki-status`) from a lit sibling run in that face's own engine (`face-engine`, `face-engine-source`): the anchor composition over its daemon canon and the slots its own wear named, beside the shared libraries and the oracle, booted from the bytes the island's own engine booted from; its session carries the tiddler writes as itself too (`FACE_LANE_CHORES`), it names its own daemon wiki (`recipe: default`) alone, and its engine closes when the face drops. ''MEASURED'' (a kept rite-walk hearth, re-founded, three faces, 2026-11-19, before each hat sealed under its own chain): a daemon slot the island stood for a lit sibling under another face's wear refused its seal `session-face-foreign`, left unsealed at the next composition, and left its control row naming a document no store held; the sibling's own wear then refused `WorkingLayerAttachRefusal` (`document-unavailable`) and stood the worn face again. So the island mints no sibling's daemon slot: a kind its own wear never named refuses `face-unresolved`, and the seed names that face as waiting for its own wear. ''MEASURED against it'' (a walked hearth, the carry lane, 2026-11-14): a lit sibling's daemon canon and its daemon working layer did not open while another face stood worn (the resolve of `bags/daemon-<F>` never settled and the store door parked), so a face's daemon layers took writes at that face's own wear alone. The kahua door now reads the chore check first: a stage, staged-meme read, lift, withdraw or carry for a lent face other than the daemon mount refuses `chore-face-not-mounted` before any document opens, and a seed lifts the worn face's items and names each other face's as waiting. ''A SIBLING PROVES FROM ITS OWN REST'': the composition mounts the worn face's persona plane alone, so a lit sibling's proof reads that sibling's plane from this vessel's own face-gated store (`proveSiblingFromRest`, `readRestingDoc`): the storage adapter's bytes load into a scratch document, never a Repo handle and never a peer. Custody binds by OPENING: each open of a face takes a fresh name on its holder (`faceOpening`, `<boot>:<n>`: a nonce the holder mints when it stands, beside a count of its opens, so a lock and re-open carries a new name and a restarted holder whose count starts over never repeats one; under a pūlama the vessel reports the pūlama's own name verbatim, carried in every standing it sends, so a lock and re-lend there reads a new opening even where the vessel never observed the face leave, and the pūlama sends that standing the moment a face shuts, `onClosed`), the face stands open before the read, the read keys its adapter call to that opening, and the same opening still stands when the proof is accepted, so a lock, or a lock and re-open, between read and acceptance leaves the plane unproved. ''THE RESIDUAL WINDOW'': under a pūlama, a lock there reaches the vessel as its next standing; until that message lands the vessel holds the old opening, and a read finishing within the message's latency is accepted. Two processes over a socket share no instant, so no check closes it. It proves nothing a read finished just before the lock would not: the lock moves no byte of the plane; the relation and every plane url take their first pin only, before the face's first admission, and no later act re-pins them; the sibling proof names no device, so admissions, edges and their revocations never enter it. A later fork or plane rewrite refuses the whole log (`fork`, `plane`), and a read landed before it arrived proves the same pins it proved before: sync staleness, independent of any lock, which the next proof reads; the anchor's frontier keeps every read at or above the head the vessel accepted. THE BOUND IS THE EVENT LOOP'S TIMER ORDER, never wall time: each caller's own timer callback alone marks its read spent, the caller accepts at its one continuation after the answer while that mark stands clear, and the decode runs synchronously after acceptance, bounded by the byte cap; a timer the loop delivers late moves the bound with it. Callers of one document and one opening share its one waiting adapter read, each under its own bound and its own acceptance, so contention never downgrades a proof; a different opening never joins; once every listener's bound spends, the opening lets the read go and the next caller reads fresh, and the stale read, when it lands, reaches no caller. Absent never means partial, and each absence reads by name: a chunk that did not read (`chunk-unread`) or carries no bytes (`chunk-empty`), a change whose parent no chunk carries (the engine queues it and keeps the earlier heads, so any dependency left unresolved refuses `torn`, the face open the whole while), a chunk that does not decode whole (the strict load refuses a truncated trailing incremental, `torn`, where an incremental load would keep the snapshot before it), more than 4096 chunks or 32 MiB (`over-cap`, checked before the concatenation allocates; the adapter lists no sizes before it loads, so the cap bounds the decode and not the adapter's own load), a malformed url, a refusal, a torn chunk. ''WHAT EACH READING LETS THE PROOF DO'' (`SIBLING_PLANE_USE`): a plane that reads joins the carried acts; ONLY a read that succeeded and found nothing at rest (`nothing-rests`) lets the carried acts answer alone, as for a fleet-joined face before its plane syncs; every other reading refuses the proof outright and logs by name, so the carried acts never stand in for a plane this read did not see whole: a plane known to rest that did not read whole (`torn`, `chunk-unread`, `chunk-empty`, `over-cap`, `url-malformed`), where restoring the missing part could expose a signed fork that makes the same proof refuse; a store that did not answer in time or refused (`bound-spent`, `refused`), since a flaky store with no byte changed would otherwise turn a plane that refuses into a carried-only proof; a face shut at the read (`face-shut`); and a lock, or a lock and re-open, while the read waited (`lock-mid-read`), whether it found a plane or an empty rest, since the carried founding would otherwise prove pins the full log refuses; the opening is read before either proof path runs and again before any proof is accepted, carried-only included. A proof carries its plane's reading with it. ''THE LIVENESS COST'': a slow or refusing store, or a face shut at that moment, refuses the sibling until the next ask (the next light, custody open or boot), and that ask reads the plane afresh under the opening it finds. ''THE CAPS' MARGIN'' (measured on the founding fixture): a founded plane carries 8 acts in 2,386 bytes, its largest act 946 bytes of JSON; at a kilobyte per act, 32 MiB holds about 33,000 acts, where a face that admits a device each month and rotates yearly for a decade signs a few hundred, so the byte cap stands two orders of magnitude clear, and the engine's compaction keeps the chunk count far under 4096. ''OWED'': that margin rests on the founding fixture's acts at full JSON size; a measurement over an aged face's real persona log (its admissions, rotations and plane acts, compacted as the engine rests them) still stands owed, and so do a walk of a sibling's staged writes across a seal-and-reopen cycle, and an integration check of a face shut at boot and opened later, covering its relay registration and the legs that depend on it. A plane whose log fails its fold never reaches the live Repo, since the read admits nothing there; the signed admit acts carried in the secret home answer beside it, as before. A store's bytes may have arrived by sync from the face's other devices: the store read keeps a peer from answering the read itself, and the signed-log fold, never the bytes' origin, authenticates each act. ''MEASURED'' (the staged two-face walk, `e2e/two-face-walk`, 2026-11-20): with the proof reading memory alone, the sibling's founding act read absent (`no-founding`), its relation null, and its daemon canon never stood (`wiki status --face 1` named no `bags/daemon-<relation>`; a lane put there refused `face-lane-holds-its-own`); reading its own rest, the walk crosses the sibling's lane write and its engine's tiddler write while face 0 stands worn, each landing in the sibling's own layers and absent to the worn face. ''OWED'': a walked hearth that crosses a sibling's lane write and its own engine's tiddler write while another face stands worn (its daemon canon and its named working slot opening within the store door's bound, its save sealing under that face's own entry), and a sibling's daemon slot minted ahead of its own wear; until the second stands, a slot its own wear never named lays at its own wear. Only the operator's hand names another face: the MCP seat marks every UDS request line and session opening as the AI seat (`"seat": "ai"`, `lares_uds.SEAT_KIND`), and the vessel reads that mark, never the absence of a session (`seat-hand.ts`): a marked call or a `seat` session naming a face other than the worn one refuses `face-hand-refused`, one naming none acts as the worn face, and a session call wearing another face meets the same refusal in the handler. The CLI's seed, import, share and vault doors carry no mark. By F7's standing ruling the mark is a designation, not a proof: on one machine, for the dogfood, authority rides by designation. A lock of one face drops its waiting saves from memory (`SealedRest.forget`), never to disk, and releases its documents' handles. ''A SIBLING'S SELVES RECORD SEALS UNDER ITS OWN HIVE'': the bag map files a lit face's selves record under that face's subject (`selvesRecordBagOf`), so its save books that face's own session, and its hive registers the record's bag beside the others it owns; a read of every face's names (`selves.read`) that stands a sibling's record as a blank seals it under that sibling's own entry. ''MEASURED'' (the operator's house, three faces lit, h1 worn, 2026-11-20): once a sibling proved from its own rest, `selves.read` stood each sibling's record as a blank; its load refused `bag-unfounded` and its save `writer-not-capable` under the sibling's hive, which left the bag out, so two saves waited in memory and the census read them as `held-save`.

''EACH FACE LIVES ONE LIFE'' (`face-lifecycle`, operator ruling 2026-11-21). One serialized lifecycle actor per face the vessel may light, the mount's beside each sibling's, owns that face's life: `dark → lighting → lit(opening) → closing → dark`, one mailbox per face and no queue shared across faces. The actor alone holds the face's opening, the atom key its island knows it by, the custody opening it lit under, the visits it admitted and the work in flight; nothing else keeps a copy. A handler reads, decides, mutates and only then runs its effects, with no await between: slow work (a light, a save drain, a worker round trip, a recompose) runs outside and returns as a message whose handler reads the state again, so an answer that lands after a close finds the face `closing` or `dark` and does nothing. Each light takes a fresh opening (`<boot nonce>:<n>`) that never repeats in the vessel's life, and a relight runs a whole close and then a light. The uds door admits each session verb and act on its face's actor before arbitration, so admitted work holds the face's close until it settles, and a face closing refuses `face-closing`; a visit's reply delivers through the target's actor, which reads `lit`, the admitted opening and the visit's own standing, and writes or refuses in that same turn; each visit rides its own request id, so no reply blocks a session. The daemon island keeps one face-change queue per face. A refused drop asks the island supervisor, which composes the island again from the actors' states alone, lighting only faces whose actor reads `lit` and never asking custody, so no cycle forms between a drop and a restart; every open releases each closing face it left out, recomposes each lit face from the entry its light proved (no custody read on the restart's path), and frees the work the restart ended. Each face's light and its first open read on their own, and the sealed store keeps one turn line per bag, so a face's drain flushes its own documents and bags and never waits behind another face's seals. Every close's return of a face to dark replays the lend, so a face whose drop answered late lights again where the lend still serves it; a refused light replays nothing (the lend would light it again at once and spin the vessel's turn on an island that keeps refusing), and a stand-down lights nothing more once it begins. Admitted work releases when its verb settles (its outcome lands, or a restart ends it), never when a caller's wait gives up. Every answer of a visit (its reply, its failure, a fault) rides the actor's one delivery turn, so each request answers exactly once. A drain that faults reaches no CLOSED: the face holds `closing`, every close waiter hears the fault, and no `face.closed` and no key lock land over unsealed saves; the next close drains again. A stand-down (a stop, a slide, a wear change, a whole lock) closes every face through its actor before anything flushes, tears down or locks, and before any next composition lights a face. ''TWO-PHASE CLOSE.'' The pūlama never locks a lent face first: it sends `closing(face, opening)` to each vessel that lends it and keeps the key; the vessel's actor stops admitting visits and work, refuses every visit not yet delivered, waits out a light and the admitted work, drains the face's saves while its sealing seat stands, tells the island, tears the host side down, and only then answers `face.closed`; the pūlama locks once every vessel it told answered. A local lock runs the same two phases inside one process, the mounted face's as every sibling's: the custody door closes the face through its actor AHEAD of its line (so no act queued behind the lock holds the drain), holds it dark (no relight between close and key), then takes its turn to lock the key and lifts the hold. A face lock that cannot land closes nothing: under a pūlama the lent holder refuses every face lock (the person's act at the pūlama), so no close runs ahead of it and no face drains for a lock that refuses. A lock whose close ran ahead runs to its end though its caller left (it locks the key or lifts the hold), and right before the key locks it reads the face still past CLOSED in its line (dark, or its saves drained and its host side down), whether or not a close ran ahead: a composition that stood again while the lock waited (a wear, or an unlock queued ahead of a lock whose face read shut at its frame) and lit the face leaves the key open and the answer an error, and the lock never closes that life inside the line its work may wait on. A whole lock reads every face of the standing composition the same way before the vessel key locks. A lock that did not lock its key lifts the hold: a sibling the lend still serves lights again, and a mount its close took dark, which no lend relights, reads lit again while its key stands open. A whole lock closes strict: a face whose drain faulted rejects that close by name, the vessel key stays open, and every hold lifts; a stop logs such a face and carries on. The mount's actor reads lit from the composition's own turn, and each sibling the island's first open lit adopts before any await, so the door gates its work and every close reaches it from the first frame; its custody opening lands as its read does. A face whose saves wait with no session to seal them holds its close (no CLOSED, no key lock, no teardown forgetting them), and the mount's drain names its documents by every anchor it wears. A pūlama's close names the face by its kept custody id first, and a custody read that faults answers nothing; a close naming an opening before the one the face lives under answers nothing and closes nothing, so no key locks under a later life, while one naming a later opening closes the older life whole before it answers. While a face closes, a close asked again tells every live session that lends the face, a late one included (a vessel back under a new session), and opens its rows to its drain (a vessel whose drain faulted drains again). A told session that ends unanswered drops, naming its vessel life: its vessel may still hold an unsealed save, so the other sessions' answers never lock over it. ''EACH LIFE NAMES ITSELF'': a vessel's store claim mints a fresh life id (`<hearth>/vessel/.life`, `vessel-life`), each pūlama hello carries it (`life: {id}`), and the pūlama reads the life its own hearth names, seating a hello whose life matches and seating any other with no life. A drop clears when the hearth names another life holding the store (the dropped life ended, and its unsealed memory with it) or when a session of the same life answers a close asked again (it came back and drained); equality alone decides, and no reading orders two lives. A drop with no life, or a hearth that names none (absent or torn), clears by the operator's forced lock alone. A close with no live lender and no drop standing locks at the next ask. The close stands until the key locked: while the holder's lock waits its turn, the face reads closing and shut to every session, so no standing sent meanwhile shows it lit, and a second answer, a re-close or a force joins that one lock; a face no session lends closes the same way through its lock's wait, and a lock that throws puts back each session still owing its answer (one that ended meanwhile drops). The pūlama's status names that clearing line. A close called off stays owed (the recovery runs a force, then a fresh light): named, not built. ''A LIFE THAT ENDED BEFORE THE CLOSE'': the pūlama remembers the lives it lent each face since that face last locked; a first close with no live lender, while a lent life ended without coming back and the hearth still names it, holds the face closing on that life as on a drop, clearing by the same line, and a hearth naming another life locks it at once. A session with no life holds nothing past its end, as before. Unbuilt beside it: the probe that reads the `.holder` presence claim across the container line (whether any life holds the store now), which waits on a two-container rehearsal. A face's custody id stays past its dark, so the lend the dark replays skips a face its pūlama names closing. While a face closes, the pūlama serves it only to the sessions its close told: a session admitted later reads it shut, and its rows refuse `face-closing`, so it neither lights the face nor stands outside the close's answers. The mount closed at its pūlama's word drains its saves under its standing session, answers, and the vessel stands again; a forced lock the mount never heard asked closes it at the next standing the same way. A vessel that never answers leaves the face closing with no clock: the pūlama names it ("face hN: the vessel has not answered its close"), and only the operator's hand confirms a forced lock (the hand door's `close` with `force`). ''THE RESIDUAL WINDOW, NAMED.'' With the vessel's answer, none: no visit body leaves after the actor took its close, and the key locks after that answer. Without it (a silent or partitioned vessel, then the forced lock), the span runs from the pūlama's `closing` to the moment that vessel hears it, its latency on the wire: a visit the vessel delivers there was admitted under an opening the vessel still read open. Two causal islands share no instant, and no check closes that span; the operator's forced-lock hand bounds it. A whole-holder `lock` drops every session at once, and acts as that hand. ''OWED'': a walked hearth that crosses a two-phase close with a visit in flight.

''Unproven.'' The session sign door signs each ask under the vessel's own chain (the worn face's anchors); whether the island's holder accepts a sibling place session's seal through that door stands unmeasured until a live walk runs it.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-share-config`>>

!! The share config ~ who reads and writes a bag, in one place

Faces do not know each other: no face's hive or catalog half names another face. The operator alone shares a bag, by TW5-native tiddlers `$:/config/lares/share/<slug>` (`share-config.ts`) whose `read` and `write` fields name faces by ''pet-name'' as a title list (`a b [[c d]]`), `*` for every face, or empty, with a `note` that says why. A filter operator refuses `share-filter-unsupported`: the tiddlers name faces, and no filter computes them.

* ''One authority, one file.'' The share tiddlers rest in the vessel's control document (the vessel's key; no face reads it) and write through to `$LAR_ROOT/config/share.tid`, which survives a re-founding and a person edits by hand. Each boot reconciles the file into the control document, and the file wins (`reconcileShareEntries`).
* ''One resolver'' (`resolveShare`) answers who reads and writes a bag, as MultiWikiServer keeps one ACL place: a house canon bag reads the floor for every face and takes no write by any share (the Kahu steward's reach decides); a custom bag stays its owner's until a share names it; a share reads and writes for the faces it names, the owner always among them, a writer reading too; a name no face carries waits, named.
* ''By reference and delegation, never a copy'' (`share-apply`, `applyShares`). Where the owner face and the grantee face both stand lit, the bag's catalog entry (the owner's ONE document's url) lifts into the grantee's catalog half and the owner's hive delegates the bag to the grantee's (`read`, or the house's writer hand for a writer). A grant whose face stands shut waits behind an opaque flag in the control document (`$:/config/lares/share-pending/<digest>`: no face, no bag, no access), and the next light lands it and clears the flag. The unlock door lands the shares as each face lights. ''A face's catalog half opens only while that face stands worn'': it rests sealed under the face's own content key, and the daemon island's one holder carries the worn face's, so the landing never asks for a lit sibling's half. A lit grantee beside the worn owner takes its grant in two acts, each by an event: the owner's hive delegates at once and the granted entries ''carry'' in the control document (`$:/config/lares/share-carry/<group>/<title>`, under the vessel's key; a wiki entry carries the wiki's recipe entry beside its canon's, so the grantee mounts the wiki), and the grantee's own wear ''lifts'' them into its own half and removes the carry (`liftCarriedShares`, run at the worn face's stand and at every landing). At each sibling's light the sibling's hive hands the worn hive the cap events it delegated to it, so the worn face reads its seat on a shared bag and on a wiki's realm from its own replica. The share door answers `landed: N grant(s)`, names how many reach their grantee's catalog at that face's next wear, and names a shut face only where one stands shut.
* ''A face shares as a cabal member.'' The multitude of one human's faces reads as a private cabal of one (operator ruling 2026-11-11: //"the multitude of faces reads as a private cabal for 1 human. Pattern integrities!"//), so a share between two faces rides the cabal grammar, a seat in a realm's Keyhive Document, never a parallel list. A `kind: "wiki"` share entry already rides it: the share door founds the wiki's private cabal once, seats each face the entry names in the realm's Document, and the workbench syncs to every seated face (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/cabal-realm#/the-workbench`>>); the entry may name a `workspace` (`/lar/workspaces/<name>`) where the workbench's plaintext mirror rests. The daemon wiki takes no share entry (`share-face-private`). ''OWED:'' a bag share still lands by the catalog lift and the owner hive's delegation above, beside the realm path rather than through it.
* ''Doors.'' `lares bag share <bag> --read <names> --write <names> [--note <why>] [--face N]`, `lares wiki share <wiki> …`, `lares bag share --list` (each share entry and the resolver's answer; `--json` prints each entry as written, its `*`, note, workspace and owner kept, beside the resolver's answer). `hN` and `--face N` resolve to that face's pet-name, so a share entry always names pet-names. No agent surface reaches `share-set` or `share-apply`.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-carrier`>>

!! The blind carrier ~ what it holds and what it never joins

* ''A carrier never joins document sync.'' It stores and serves sealed values by address under the house's own carriage protocol.
* ''Its causal index'' builds from clear parts alone: the bag locator; key version records with their parents; manifests with their entries; each checkpoint's address with the addresses it covers; each change's address with its parent addresses; each re-seal record's replaced version and address with the replacement's; and the retention pins its contract names.
* It verifies an address against bytes and a record id against a clear part. It verifies no signature, since every signature rides sealed; the carriage protocol's own proof decides who may deposit.
* It may drop what no pin holds. It cannot forge an entry a reader accepts, and it cannot move a sealed value between bags, roles or references.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/what-the-code-holds`>>

!! What the code holds

''Lockbox delivery: the store stands built, and minting waits on a session signer.'' A bag's content key reaches its readers by lockbox alone (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/cap-tier#/key-delivery`>>). The lockbox store, the chunk index and the holder's seat stand on every unlocked node vessel. No content key mints on a production vessel until a production path opens a session signer.

''Built: a production path reaches each.''
* ''The sealed value'' (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/sealed-chunk.ts`): seal, open, the address, the authorized address and every refusal of #/the-sealed-value.
* ''The content-addressed store'' (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/ciphertext-cas.ts`): a private body seals and opens through the sealed value alone, as a `blob` bound to its plaintext digest. No opener returns unauthenticated bytes, and no key derives from content or from a Nexus secret.
* ''The walker's carriage'' (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/walk-carry.ts`): a walker seals each carried document under that document's own key and opens it against its own receipt, which names the address, the document's digest and the key. Carries for one hearth run in turn, and a document the hearth already serves back deposits nothing twice. The hearth verifies the address and holds no key (`packages/lararium-node/src/hosting-carry.ts`).
* ''The verified read'' (`packages/lararium-node/src/node-cas.ts`, `cas-wire.ts`, `bulb-read-face.ts`; `packages/lararium-mesh/src/cas-transit.ts`): a holder serves a block, and takes a local hit, only where the bytes still recompute to their address; a block altered at rest reads as a block the holder lacks, on the sealed tier and on the clear one.
* ''The content-key holder's seat'' (`packages/lararium-keyhive/src/content-key-seat.ts`, `operator-daemon-behavior.ts`; `packages/lararium-node/src/node-daemon-island.ts`, `sealed-store-island.ts`): a node vessel's daemon island stands the holder beside its Keyhive identities once it unlocks, over the vessel's own `sealed/` and `cad/` tiers, and the holder leaves with the identity it stands beside. The island owns its document and keeps no other store, so it reads its storage directory off the owned document (`islandStorageDirOf`). The island answers the `sealed-*` verbs there, and an island with no identity standing refuses each by name.
* ''The roll's subscription'' (the same seat): the content-key roll subscribes to the island's own seat rolls, so `lares edge settle` hands each roll it answers to it. A bag no key ever served owes no roll, and settles.
* ''The provider's tracked chunks'' (`packages/lararium-keyhive/src/keyhive-provider.ts`; `packages/lararium-node/src/sealed-store-fs.ts`): each identity writes the Keyhive chunks it tracks through the node's rest, under an opaque shelf name, before the call that sealed one returns. An identity that stands again finds what it held.
* ''The browser island's rest and its seat'' (`packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-sealed-store.ts`, `daemon-island-start.ts`): the browser's daemon island, the thread the vessel worker spawns, stands its Keyhive identities, so the holder stands there. The island rests lockboxes, records, index entries, sealed values, the cleartext-local tier and its tracked Keyhive chunks on IndexedDB shelves of its own, each write answering once its transaction commits, and its start hands that shore to its behaviour, which seats the holder.
* ''The browser's capability record and its session door'' (`packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-island-caps.ts`, `open-browser-daemon-vm.ts`, `daemon-island-start.ts`; the answers on the mesh floor, `packages/lararium-mesh/src/capability-record.ts` `answerIslandAsks`, the book `session-sign-book.ts`; the island's door `packages/lararium-keyhive/src/island-session-proof.ts` `islandSessionDoor`): the browser composes its record as the node composes its own, receipt and cas at construction, and a lift that seats `custodyWrite` and `sessionSign` while its custody stands open under a joined face's Nexus session, and takes both back at the lock, dropping the session's signer. The vessel worker answers its daemon island's asks through that record, and the island asks its session door there. A vessel at its floor answers `session:sign` with `signer-unavailable`, as a herm does. The door signs a call only where a session of this vessel booked it. The vessel worker's sealed rest books its seals under the lift's own session (below); no other browser path books a call yet, so every other mint on a browser vessel still refuses `signer-unavailable` (#/owed, the session proof).
* ''The document store's sealed rest under each vessel's Repo'' (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/sealed-rest.ts` `composeSealedRest`, `vesselDocStoreDoor`, `answerDocStoreAsks`, `docStorePortCell`; `sealed-doc-store.ts`; the record's `docStore` member, `capability-record.ts`; the island's answer, `packages/lararium-keyhive/src/operator-daemon-behavior.ts` `docStoreDoor`; the node, `packages/lararium-node/src/open-node-vessel.ts`, `node-daemon-island.ts`, `worker-handle.ts`, `uds-channel.ts` `beforeSignerDrops`; the browser, `packages/lararium-browser/src/open-browser-vessel.ts`, `daemon-island-start.ts`). Each vessel's own Repo stands on the sealed rest, and the holder in its daemon island seals and opens across a door that carries a body and a sealed value, never a key: a seal books its ask in the vessel's session sign book under the session that booked the change, so the island signs through that session alone; an open answers the body and the face whose session signed its manifest; the island asks the vessel for a bag's live document to cut a from-now admission's fresh base, which each daemon island hands its seat (`freshBase`; the browser's in `daemon-island-start.ts`). Every save books its change's own writer, read off the save's own bytes (`chunkActors`, `SealedRest.watch`): a change this replica made books the session standing (the node: the session whose call stands; the browser: the vessel's unlocked session while its lift stands), and a peer's change books its peer writer and rests as received under the session that holds the rest; each save signs under its booked session. A save no session booked refuses `save-unbooked` and waits in memory, readable and never written; a compaction leaves the sealed chunks its waiting snapshot replaces at rest; the next session that books its document seals it (the node: a session's next call; the browser: the next lift). The lock flushes the Repo and the store before any booking or signer drops (the node's session close and switch, the browser's lock), and the node's lock first seals its face's waiting saves under the leaving session. The stand-down flushes the same way: the verb socket's seat closes first, each session's flush lands while the daemon island still answers, and the rest's last flush names what still waits (`SealedRest.standDown`; `packages/lararium-node/src/main.ts`, `uds-channel.ts` `serveUdsFront`). A save no session can seal rests nowhere new, and its change returns by sync from the replica that holds it once a session stands again. A clear chunk the engine wrote before its bag mapped sealed reads for the bag's own face and leaves rest at the first booked save; a sealed value under a document the map names no bag for refuses `bag-unmapped` and never reaches the engine as clear. Each vessel maps the worn face's wiki bags (wiki, working, draft) sealed while that face's Nexus chain stands (the node's dial, the browser's lift), each by every name its document carries (`sealedBagMap`): the layer the mount resolved, each document the daemon island's slot resolver announces before any write lands in it (`announceSlotDoc`, `docstore:slot`; `packages/lararium-keyhive/src/operator-daemon-behavior.ts`), then the catalog's entry. A document that mapped only after it rested clear seals at once under the standing session, and its clear chunks leave rest (`sealMapped`). ''A restart's hydration'': a sealed bag's document hydrates only once the face's chain stands again (the node's dial, the browser's lift). A load that refuses only because its map, its session or its holder has not stood yet waits for the next move of the map (`mapMoved`: the dial composing, a slot named, a lift, a call) and reads again, so no sealed document strands unavailable ahead of the dial; no clock decides the wait, and only the rest's stand-down ends it (`endHydration`), each waiting load then refusing by its own name (`bag-unmapped` where no map stood). Every value it hydrates passes the face gate before a byte moves. The restart run (`tests/e2e/sealed-wiki-restart.test.ts`, standing, no env gate; one run 2026-11-05 in a PID namespace under a scratch root) reads the marker back whole and finds it clear nowhere outside the disk projection (T2's daemon half, built on the node). ''No wiki island keeps a store'': its manifest names none, so its Repo relays in memory through the vessel's (`packages/lararium-tw5/src/sovereign-kernel.ts`; the pool, `vessel-island-pool-core.ts`), the pool opens every document it grants the island on the vessel's Repo before the island binds, and the island settles its last heads by receipt before it acks a teardown. ''The daemon island's own store'' (its owned document's, or its Repo's where none stands: the node's `packages/lararium-node/src/sovereign-island-model.ts`, the browser's `browser-sovereign-island-model.ts`) stands behind `islandRestGuard`: before it rests a byte of a document it asks the vessel (`rest:ask`, the record's `restsSealed` member), overlapping acts on one document sharing one ask, and a document the vessel's rest holds sealed stays in the island's memory and reaches rest by sync, sealed in the vessel's store; the first sealed answer sweeps the clear copy the island held. ''The node's own session'': the dial's presentation opens a session of the worn face at the Nexus it dials, and that session sits seated in each book a lift stands while the dial stands (`standingSessionSeat`), so a change written with no call in flight books it.
* ''A public bag's published key'' (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/published-bag-key.ts`; the document store's published path, `sealed-doc-store.ts`): a public or crossroads bag publishes one lookup, 32 random bytes read as text, and its content key derives by HKDF-SHA-256 from that lookup and the bag's own id. The store rests the bag's values sealed under that key in the one sealed-value format, and opens them for whoever holds the lookup, with no session, no face and no holder; a private bag's sealed value opens under no published key. The key grants reading alone: it signs nothing and mints no record.
* ''The direct holder's rest'' (`packages/lararium-node/src/owned-store.ts`): the holder that opens a store with no vessel standing stands no daemon island and no session, so it seals nothing. It stands on the document store with `refuseClearOverSealed`: a document whose rest holds a sealed value refuses its clear save and its read by name (`bag-unmapped`), and every other document rests as the engine hands it.

''Specified in code, each with its named caller.''
* ''A mint'': the founding, the roll's successor, the join, the from-now admission and the hand-over (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/content-key-holder.ts`), the lockbox wrap (`lockbox-wrap.ts`; `packages/lararium-keyhive/src/keyhive-lockbox.ts`, `keyhive-provider.ts`) and the records they sign (`sealed-records.ts`). Each runs whole over real Keyhive identities and a founded island in the suites. In production each refuses `signer-unavailable`: a session signs every record, and no production path opens a session signer yet. Caller: the session open of <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/face-proof#/the-key-hierarchy`>>, which hands the island its `sessionProof` (the acting session's signer, and the chain check that reads who may write a bag).
* ''The memory section and the import door'' (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/memory-section.ts`; `content-key-holder.ts` `cutMemory` and `importMemory`; `sealed-records.ts`, the `memory-import` kind; the island's `sealed-memory-cut`, `sealed-memory-door` and `sealed-memory-import` verbs, `packages/lararium-keyhive/src/operator-daemon-behavior.ts`). Each runs whole in the suites, over the modelled key shore and over a founded island's real Keyhive identities. The custody door's `backup` frame places the cut on a standing vessel (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/what-the-code-holds`>>), so the cut stands built. The restore's import act (`bundle.import`) places the door and the import on a standing vessel (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/the-restore`>>). An import signs a record, so on a production vessel it refuses `signer-unavailable` until a session signer stands; the rehearsal runs it whole on scratch roots with a session proof composed.
* ''The store'' (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/sealed-store-rest.ts`; `packages/lararium-node/src/sealed-store-fs.ts`): lockboxes, records and index entries at rest, by names their own bytes derive. The holder reads it at each unlock; it holds nothing until a first mint.
* ''The vessel's register door'' (`packages/lararium-node/src/seal-carrier-federation.ts` `registerSealedCarrier`, `ciphertext-cas-seal.ts` `registerSealedBody`): the vessel registers a body the island sealed, by its address, and refuses bytes that rest nowhere, fail their address or read as no sealed value. Caller: the `cad-seal` verb's compose hunk; the verb as composed still asks for a key no caller hands it, and so refuses.
* ''The key lineage's base rule and the roll for each founding'' (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/content-key-lineage.ts`).
* ''The raises a vessel counts'': the seat counts a raise where the island's own count answered it, and reads the vessel's ledger through `countedRaises` where a composer hands that in. Caller: a door beside the dyad's own, from the island to the vessel's fold of its fleet board. Until it stands, a vessel that restarts hands its kept rolls back through the `sealed-rolls` verb before it reads a removal's version.
* ''A roll's replacement and its re-seal'' (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/content-key-holder.ts` `resealBlob` and `judgeReseals`; `sealed-records.ts` the `re-seal` kind and `judgeReseal`; `content-key-lineage.ts` `nearestRemoval`): a body under a version the nearest removal within the head's span rolled past, in that same span, seals again under the head and signs its re-seal record, every refusal landing before a join of the heads mints and nothing resting until every signature lands; a span with no removal refuses `head-not-rolled`, a body of another span alone `replaced-other-span`, and a body the roll's side already names `value-current`. Blobs replace today. Caller: none yet; the door that asks a replacement after a roll lands with the roll's own surface.
* ''The admission act's call'': `sealed-admit` mints the from-now version and `sealed-hand-over` hands a shareable span. ''The same admission entered again repairs its span'': it answers the version its act opened, by the act's causal id and never a clock, mints no second one, and cuts that span's fresh base once where a stop between the version's record and its base left none (`content-key-holder.ts` `admitFromNow`). Caller: the face join, once it seats a device. A from-now admission also asks the document store for its fresh base: the seat passes a `freshBase` port through (`content-key-seat.ts`), and `freshBasePort` (`sealed-doc-store.ts`) cuts one from a live document. Each daemon island, the node's and the browser's, hands its seat that port over the bag's live document, which it asks of the vessel across the document store's door, and beside it `readsAsFreshBase`: a span opens on a cut that reads as one founding change alone, and any other cut reads as no base (`fresh-base-unavailable`).
* ''The document store's sealed rest'' (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/sealed-doc-store.ts`; `content-key-holder.ts` `sealValues` and `openValue`; `packages/lararium-keyhive/src/content-key-seat.ts` `sealValues`): it wraps a Repo's storage adapter, seals a sealed bag's saves through the holder, rests a pointer in the engine's own rest, and gates every read on the session's face. Each runs whole in the suites, over the modelled key shore and over IndexedDB on a worker's thread in Chromium. Caller: the composer under each vessel's Repo (built, above).
* ''The outbox'' (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/sealed-store-rest.ts`): the `sealed-hand-over`, `sealed-parcels` and `sealed-serve` verbs seal each parcel whole there under the outbox key an unlock handed the daemon island, its reader inside the seal, under a random name, and answer its name and a count; with no key handed each refuses `outbox-shut` before any key act. Nothing reads a parcel out. Caller: the member lane of step four. The outbox key's record, its hand-off and the crash law read at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/custody#/what-the-code-holds`>>.

''No per-Nexus shared secret stands'' (operator ruling). The code holds no convergence secret, no keyring record and no delivery of one, and no reader derives from one. A bag's content key reaches its readers by lockbox alone, and the `cad-seal` verb reads no keyring.

