<<~ ahu slot=`#/owed`>>

!! What the later steps owe

* ''The session proof'': the session open that hands the daemon island a signer and the chain check. The node's lift seats its door, and the session socket books each call it places. The browser's lift seats the same door over its joined face's Nexus session, and its book holds only the document store's seals, booked under the vessel's own unlocked session: no other browser path books a call, since a tab's own session and its own unlock wait on the browser lift's third slice (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/pono/browser-vessel-lift#/the-slices`>>). Until a tab's session books the sealed act it places, no content key mints on a browser vessel.
* ''A removal that leaves out the founding vessel'': the vessel that founded a face's key group holds that group through an identity no device edge names, so a raise over it names no seat a roll can leave out, and it keeps receiving keys. The PersonaGroup stands as a Keyhive group, so any other seat's revocation rolls every bag the group reaches; the founder's seat stands upstream of every hand and leaves only by a fresh group (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/kapae#/keys`>>; roadmap loop `K12`).
* ''Step three, the two rulings the composer stands on.'' ''R1, which bags rest sealed'' (operator ruling, 2026-10-12): //"the projected files-on-disk memes should be clear-text… All other stores besides the literal live wikis in memory should read encrypted/protected (and public/crossroads, etc, available as invitees have the 'public keys or lookups')"//, the scope narrowed the same day to //"the live wiki and CAD contents"//. Every wiki content store (each bag's document, node and browser) and the content-addressed store rest sealed: a private bag under its bag's content key, a public or crossroads bag under its published key, which opens for whoever holds its lookups. Cleartext rests only in the disk projection and in the live wiki in memory. ''R2, who signs a save the Repo makes on its own timer'' (house ruling, overrulable by the operator, read from //"a session signs or nothing mints"//): every change books the session that wrote it, and the store's save signs under that booked session; the lock ritual flushes the Repo before it drops any signer or overwrites a key; a save no session booked (a peer's change that arrived unsealed, a change after the lock) refuses `save-unbooked` by name and stays in memory, never written clear and never dropped silent, and the next unlock's session books and seals it. The composer stands built on both (#/what-the-code-holds).
* ''Step three, the house decisions on R1's reach'' (2026-11-05, each overrulable by the operator). ''T1, boards stay clear and a face's own names seal'': the fleet board, the edge ledger and the other signed-act boards hold signed public acts, which a locked floor folds in the clear by the waking-floor law (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/waking-floor`>>), never wiki content, and R1's scope reads //"only the live wiki and CAD contents"//; the fleet board and the edge ledger rest signed edge acts alone, read by their kind and not measured byte by byte. The persona plane follows the operator's ruling of 2026-11-18, //"This sounds like a pono design to move forwards with"//: the plane keeps signed public acts alone, which the locked floor folds, and a face's two own names (its pet-name and its declared Handle) rest apart from it in that face's ''selves record'', a document of its own sealed under the face's key group through the one sealed-value format, its locator derived from the face's group and never from a name, opened after the lift and never read at the floor (design: lar:///ha.ka.ba/kahua/memory/research/persona-selves-sealed). The split follows the store's grain: a rest class belongs to a whole document, never to a field. One face holds one selves record, and no record names two faces. While the record stays owed, a name refuses onto a plane that rests `clear` (`packages/lararium-node/src/persona-selves-plane.ts`), so no plane rests a private body. ''T2, the daemon bag, the catalog and the island-owned Repos seal and read after the lift'': they hold wiki content, so they rest sealed, the boot reads none of them at the floor, and the lift opens and reads them. Built: the direct holder never writes clear over a sealed rest (#/what-the-code-holds). Built (P1): a vessel's document store keeps each document's rest class for the process, so a map that drops never writes clear over a sealed rest. Built (V2, V3): each island's own store rests no document the vessel seals, and the map names every slot document its resolver answers (#/what-the-code-holds); V3's cause, measured: the map read the catalog's entry alone, a wiki's working and draft slots stand under no catalog entry (the face's derived address, a binding record), and the seat that lands moves the working slot to the face's own document while the vessel's mounted layer still names the floor's. ''U1, the house decision on which key seals a vessel-wide document'' (2026-11-05, overrulable by the operator): no vessel-wide content key mints, since one would make a shared secret (S) against the ruling that a shared act never makes a shared secret. The CATALOG dissolves into a per-face catalog naming that face's bags, sealed under that face's own key (D), and a floor catalog naming public bags alone, readable at the floor (F). The DAEMON BAG splits the same way: the herm's floor core (the public, signed code and config the floor runs) stays floor-public and signed (F), and each face's daemon wiki content seals under that face (D). A herm wears no face, so it reads the F halves alone; the boot reads F alone at the floor, and each face's D halves open at that face's lift. Reason: a face's custody records seal under its own key, and a node holds S only for records it names, so a document that names several faces splits by face rather than sealing under a key no single face owns. Built on the node (step one of the split): the daemon bag's D half. The daemon wiki's working and draft slots register under the lit, proved face's hive and map sealed under that face, both read off one derivation (`wornFaceWikiBags`, `packages/lararium-mesh/src/register-bags.ts`), since every bag the rest seals the face must register: measured, a map that sealed the slots while the register set named the user wiki's alone refused each seal `writer-not-capable` (the island's standing and its content-key seat read `registerBags`), held the saves `save-unsealed` in memory and stalled the anon-veil lift on a flush that never answered. The verb outcome ledger (`bags/control/outcomes/`) and its effect index rest in the vessel control document (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/control-home.ts`, written through `packages/lararium-tw5/src/verb-vm.ts`), which no recipe composes and no mirror names; the CLI's wait (`packages/lararium-node/src/uds-channel.ts`) and the main thread's own island ask (`island-verb-ask.ts`) read and hear that store, and the host's mounted working layer follows the island's onto the face's document at the seat (`rebindWorkingLayer`). At the floor the slot stands on the floor's own document, which holds no private content, and the lift burns it. The restart run (one run, 2026-11-05, PID namespace, scratch root) finds the marker clear in no store outside the disk projection, the daemon document included. The daemon wiki's canon bag and the daemon document stay F. Built on the node (step two of the split, its first half): the catalog's two halves (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/catalog-view.ts`). An entry's class names its half (`catalogRowHalf`): the registry's own pointers, the joined corpora, the live system canon, the realm plane's entries, the daemon wiki's floor half and the floor's own vessel-only slot documents rest on F; every other entry (a user bag, a wiki and its recipe, a face plane, the kahua stage, a title nothing classes) rests on the face. The face's catalog takes an address its secret alone derives (`faceCatalogDocUrl`; another face's secret refuses `face-catalog-foreign`), and it rests sealed under the lit face (`FACE_CATALOG_BAG_ID`, registered and sealed beside the daemon wiki's slots): the boot reads the floor's catalog alone, and once the face proves it EXPECTS the face (the map names the derived document sealed, and each face entry the boot writes waits in the view's memory, never on the floor nor in its history), stands the daemon island and its holder, then opens the sealed catalog, binds it, lifts the floor's face entries into it and asks the island to register every bag it names beyond the set its open carried (`daemon:face-bags`). A floor entry never waits on the face's catalog (the accessor reads the floor alone for it), and the island's boot reads the face's plane off its mounted layer. Measured (one restart run, 2026-11-05, PID namespace, scratch root): the face catalog's shard decodes to no entry at rest while the floor's decodes to its entries, and the restart reads the marker back through it; with the bag held out of the sealed set, the same run decoded five clear entries. The node's one catalog handle stands as the view (`makeCatalogView`): reads merge the face's entries over the floor's, each write lands on its entry's half (one change over a canon entry and a wiki entry lands each on its own), and at the proved face's boot, after its boot dial, the floor's face entries move into the face's catalog (`liftFaceCatalogRows`), so the floor names public entries alone. The exits hand out the scope-right view: the daemon island's and each wiki island's grant carry `faceCatalogUrl` beside the floor's url, every island accessor and register act reads it (`makeCatalogAccessor`'s third argument; CREATE's entry lands in the face's catalog), and every accessor this process opens over the floor reads the face it stands lit (`standLitFaceCatalog`), so a standing CLI door reads F with the lit face's D while the direct store door, a herm and a locked floor read F alone. K20 reads every document the store rests for the outcome ledger (one 2026-11-05 run, PID namespace, scratch root: one document holds outcomes, the realm feed's among them, none linked). Built (the seat-to-lift gap): a lit face's working slot reads its wiki's realm workbench, founded at the wiki's first stand as a private cabal of one, and a vessel that wears no face stands no working layer and refuses `no-face` (`packages/lararium-keyhive/src/slot-doc-resolver.ts`; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/cabal-realm#/the-workbench`>>). Built: a direct store door with no vessel standing reads a face's planes off the face's own proof (the persona plane off the lit catalog's entry or the founding's bootstrap, its records projected against the face's lineage; the intents plane off the face's signed plane act), so intent, kahua, promote, swarm and the fleet join answer stand at the floor (`loadIntentFace`, `device-admit`). Built: each face reads and registers through its own view (`faceViewBook`): a sibling lit by `lightFace`, or a relay face, through the floor with its own D, which it holds in memory and re-derives from its proof at each lighting. The node run (`tests/e2e/face-catalog-split.test.ts`, one run 2026-11-05): a lit CREATE lands in the face's catalog, the floor's catalog at rest names no face-class entry, and a locked restart boots, binds its rendezvous and opens no face's catalog. Owed: a sibling face's D sealed under its own chain (the map seals under one face, the worn one); the draft slot of a lit face before its seat (it still rests on the DID-keyed floor document); a verb's answer at the locked floor (measured: `nexus realm-bags` read `outcome-unknown` after 30s, since a faceless floor attaches no working slot for the outcome ledger); the two-face node-vessel and herm-on-F runs; the browser's twin of both halves. The browser's wiki islands keep their own store unguarded (no vessel answers their rest ask yet). ''T3, the stand-down flushes'': built (#/what-the-code-holds); reason: a save waiting at a stand-down must never be lost, and the session that booked it seals it while the island still answers. ''T4, the true writer'': built; reason: only a change made here books a local session, and a peer's change rests as received with its writer carried. The manifest's sealed part names the writer under its signature (`session:<session key>` or `peer:<actor>`), so it reads off the record once the book is gone. A change made while a face's session stands open, with no call in flight, books that seated session. ''V6'': built; the node's dial session stands seated while its dial stands, so a save waits `save-unbooked` only where no face stands unlocked. Owed: the crash test (a kill seam no product path reaches) that proves no loss across a crash for a booked edit. ''T5, a public bag's published key'': built; owed (V4): the bag charter's published lookup field, the vessel's bag map naming a public bag by its lookup, and a manifest the bag's publisher signs beside each published value, opened under it alone; until then a lookup holder could rest a forged value. ''T6, the browser island's fresh base'': built; a fresh base's checkpoint record rests after its value and manifest, so a stop between them leaves no stood base and the repair cuts it again. The browser's lock unmaps the face and its bags and drops the island holder's keys at once, and its flush stands down ahead of the daemon worker; the document store's door crosses a shared holder, and a seal no island answers waits `save-unsealed`, never thrown away. `readsAsFreshBase` reads each cut where a span opens (V7, built). Still owed: the cleartext-local (staged) tier on both vessels, sealed under its bag's content key (V5: `packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-sealed-store.ts` `staged`, `packages/lararium-node/src/sealed-store-fs.ts`; its writer, `sealed-open`, and its reader, `sealed-seal`, name no bag at the shelf today), and the orphan values a refused or timed-out seal leaves, collected by causal id (V7).
* ''Step three, what stays owed.'' A vessel whose face stands at its place, with no Nexus chain, maps no bag sealed, since only a Nexus-anchored session signs a sealed record. A tab's own session (#/owed, the session proof); the door or verb that asks a roll's replacement and so calls the re-seal record (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/rulings`>>); each face's selves record (T1), mapped sealed under the face's key group on both vessels, with the persona plane's composer handing the plane that record's real rest in place of a hard-coded `clear`; and the cleartext-local tier, which a staged body and an opened one still rest in. A plane's rest seals through this format alone, and no second format seals one document beside it.
* ''Step four'': the carriage protocol that publishes and fetches sealed values and clear record parts, the carrier's index, and its pins. It takes from step two: the store's values exactly as they rest (a lockbox, a record and an index entry each travel as their resting bytes); the parcel, which rides the key group's member lane and never a carrier; the vessel's outbox, where each parcel rests until that lane takes it, and the reader that empties it; and the `sealed-parcels`, `sealed-receive` and `sealed-serve` verbs at each end of that lane. The sealed set a vessel registers lives in memory, so a restart re-registers from the `cad/` tier there.
* ''Step five'': a public bag served at the floor of a locked node.
* ''Sync across epochs'': the act that re-authors a late change onto a fresh base, its record, and the door that shows a late change to a person.
* ''The join of rival foundings'': two foundings of one bag stand apart under two locators. Their join needs a rule for the locator the joined lineage carries, and a `fresh` base.
* ''The join of unlike spans'': a writer that holds heads of unlike spans joins them onto a `fresh` base. A base cut from one span's live document would drop the other span's writes in silence, since no engine merge crosses a span boundary, so the join waits on the act that re-authors a late change (sync across epochs, above). Until then it refuses by name (`join-across-spans`) and the fork keeps showing (house reading).
* ''A contact card for every seat'': a wrap seats a reader by its card, and a vessel holds the cards of the devices it saw summoned. A grant that carried the cards of the seats already standing would leave no reader unserved at a mint.
* ''The browser shore'': the Archive writer rides browser custody's slot tree and one sealed writer through its IndexedDB `CustodyIo` port (`packages/lararium-browser/src/browser-custody.ts`), and the island's rest and its seat stand (#/what-the-code-holds). The island still owes its session proof, so its holder mints nothing until one reaches it.
* ''Doors for a person'': a command that seals and opens a body in one act, and the readings that show a fork, a rival founding, an unserved reader and an uncounted version.
* ''Range reads'': a verified read of one segment out of a large value, without hashing the whole.

